Everything in the fix list is edited, but I could not run `npm test` at all. The first Bash call worked, then Bash was denied for every later call, so no tests ran. I did not try to work around the denial. I have no `total/pass/fail` lines for either run, and nothing I wrote has been executed or syntax-checked. Please run `npm test` twice before relying on any of it.

**Files changed (all under `/root/bench-runs/h4-610ab0b5/test/`)**
- `support/harness.mjs`:
  - removed the stale comment;
  - `request()` has a 5 s timeout that destroys the request with a clear error;
  - terse names renamed.
- `support/fake-dom.mjs`: the public dir now comes from `fileURLToPath(new URL('../../public/', import.meta.url))`, and the terse names are renamed (the page handle is `page`).
- `support/fixtures.mjs` (new): holds the shared pieces:
  - `BASE_BOOKING`, `JSON_HEADERS` and `ROOM_NAMES`;
  - `useServer`, `expectError`, `listAll` (asserts 200), `postOk` and `seedBookings`.
- `support/page-helpers.mjs` (new): shared page-test helpers, including `assertFetchOptions` for item 11.
- `server-api.test.mjs`: now imports the shared helpers. The sort fixture has three more bookings on 2026-11-05, so start, end and id order all differ, and the full sorted array is checked. The old empty-result date probe moved to 11-06.
- `server-rules.test.mjs`:
  - imports the shared helpers;
  - `postChunked` has a 5 s timeout and `res.on('error', reject)`;
  - added the two 415 cases with a single latin1 0xA0 byte (`application/json\xa0` and `\xa0application/json`), sent over a raw socket with `Connection: close` and Content-Length;
  - added room values `toString`, `constructor`, `__proto__` and `hasOwnProperty`;
  - the NBSP/BOM title literals are now explicit `\u00a0\ufeff` escapes, because I could not confirm the invisible characters survived the rewrite.
- `server.test.mjs`:
  - imports the shared helpers;
  - EADDRINUSE-only catch through a new `tryHoldPort` helper;
  - new PORT=65535 test: it holds the port, then asserts exit 1, one stderr line, and a line different from the PORT=65536 line;
  - the pipelined drain test is now table-driven over 415, 413, 404 `not found`, 405 and 404 `booking not found`, each with a 2 MB body followed by `GET /api/rooms`.
- `page.test.mjs`: shortened to the load, rooms, list and filter tests. It also gets a 204-list case and GET-options checks.
- `page-booking.test.mjs` (new): holds the booking and cancel tests. I split them out because the old `page.test.mjs` plus the new cases would have passed the 600-line limit. `npm test` picks it up through the existing `test/*.test.mjs` glob. It covers items 7 to 11:
  - the filtered refetch after 201, and after 204 and 404 on cancel;
  - the ids 3 and 8 cancel test;
  - POST answered with 204 and an unreadable body giving `Request failed.`, with Book enabled and no refetch;
  - the `deepStrictEqual` body comparison and the key-by-key header check;
  - DELETE and GET options limited to `method`, `headers` and `body`.

**Things to check on the first run**
- The 0xA0 cases (item 2) have no fallback. If Node's parser rejects the byte before the server sees it, you will get a 400 with no JSON body, and those two cases need dropping.
- The PORT=65535 test fails if the server's bind-failure line is identical to its PORT-validation line, or if the bind line is the `cannot read data file` message.
- The two 204 cases (POST and list GET) expect `Request failed.`. If `public/app.js` calls `json()` on a 204 and reports `Could not reach the server.` instead, that is a spec violation and the test should stay.
- A 204 reply to a list GET with an unreadable body also expects `Request failed.`.
- If the server closes the connection early on the 2 MB 413, 404 or 405 pipelined cases, the drain tests will fail. That would also be a spec violation.

There were no unclear points in the spec.
